Author-Name: Belleflamme, Paul
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, LIDAM/CORE, Belgium
Author-Name: Peitz, Martin
Author-Workplace-Name: University of Mannheim
Title: Network goods, price discrimination, and two-sided platforms
Abstract: A monopolist selling a network good to heterogeneous users is shown to become a two-sided platform if it can condition prices on some user characteristics or if it cannot but induces user self-selection by offering screening contracts. This shows that the availability of sophisticated pricing instruments is essential to make a platform two-sided, not the ability to distinguish separate user groups. The use of freemium strategies (which consists of offering a base version at zero price and a premium version at a positive price) emerges as a special case of versioning.
Classification-JEL: D21, D42, L12, L14
Keywords: Network goods ; two-sided platforms ; group pricing ; versioning ; freemium
